    "updated_at": "2019-05-08T17:54:18Z",
    "body": "It's just a minor thing: in general DOM attributes or other structure are meant to be under the control of the app developer, not the components. App developers should only see attributes that they added, and in particular ARIA DOM attributes should be a way for app developers to override components' default behavior.\r\n\r\nRight now there is no API for allowing components to state their own default behavior with regard to accessible roles/states/properties. (Or, similarly, manipulate their parents' default behavior.) So component authors have to add attributes to control it, which is suboptimal, but is the best we have right now. I'm just referring to the fact that in the future, we'll be able to give advice of the sort \"don't modify your parents' attributes\", without the caveat of \"except that's the only way you can do accessible roles/states/properties, so I guess it's OK to do it there\".\r\n\r\nAnd yeah, my \"I don't think that's true in general\" was referring to that fact that you'd indeed still modify your parents, just not via attributes.",
