    "created_at": "2019-05-08T23:45:40Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-05-08T23:45:40Z",
    "body": "I'm supportive of this as well. Mostly, I have seen workarounds for  restriction by triggering a custom event when the child is connected, the parent element will capture it, stop it, and call a method of it to provide a unregistration hook, this hook is a function that the child will have to invoke whenever its disconnectedCallback is invoked. This cover cases like `<x-list><x-item>` where you need some sort of registration mechanism. Passing the parentNode into the hook makes things simpler, and user-land coordinations can be implemented easier.",
