    "created_at": "2019-05-21T07:29:37Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-05-21T07:29:37Z",
    "body": "That's an interesting point. If that is indeed important we'd need to always pass parent and an additional boolean that indicates whether it's the parent of the node that caused the disconnection to happen.\r\n\r\nI think the setup can occur when you have A with child B with child C. Then A removes B and in its callback B removes child C. When the disconnection callback for C comes due to the removal of B from A it no longer has a parent, but its former parent also wasn't the parent of the node that caused the disconnection to happen.\r\n\r\nI'm not really convinced that scenario is important though as we don't have a general remove callback so it seems weird to give it special consideration here whereas otherwise you can move nodes among disconnected subtrees freely without such notices.",
